WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Model-only filtering is not enough for wormable prompt injection. Filters
can help, but NIST and OWASP both emphasize that current mitigations do not
provide perfect prevention.

2
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:19.700
The stronger posture is system-level defense: assume untrusted content may
contain instructions, and design the application so those instructions do
not gain authority.

3
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:30.260
The first boundary is instruction versus data. System policy, developer
instructions, user intent, retrieved evidence, tool output, and memory
should not be treated as equal sources of authority.

4
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:39.380
The application should explicitly label and structure those categories. It
should also validate model outputs before they reach tools, files, messages,
or business decisions.

5
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.260
The second boundary is observation versus action.

6
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:49.620
A model may need to read untrusted content to summarize or classify it, but
that does not mean it should act on instructions found inside that content.

7
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.660
High-impact actions should require deterministic checks, user confirmation,
or policy approval.

8
00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:00.500
A secure workflow can let the model draft an action while preventing it from
executing the action without authorization.

9
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:08.100
The third boundary is context isolation. Separate tasks, tenants, users, and
trust levels should not share uncontrolled memory.

10
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:18.820
Content from one workspace should not quietly become instructions for
another. If memory is used, it needs provenance, expiration, review, and a
way to quarantine suspicious entries.

11
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:26.660
The same principle applies to summaries, drafts, extracted action items, and
generated labels that may later be retrieved as facts.

12
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:39.620
Useful controls include source allowlists, retrieval filters, output
schemas, destination allowlists, egress limits, rate limits, canary content,
audit logs, and connector-specific permissions.

13
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.020
None of these is a silver bullet. Together they make the system less
brittle.

14
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:50.420
The goal is layered containment: when a model misreads untrusted text, the
application should still preserve control.

15
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.660
Express as many safety decisions as possible in deterministic application
code, where they can be tested and audited.
